  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Dacrymyces corticioides is a gelatinous jelly fungus belonging to the Dacrymycetaceae family, commonly found decomposing wood in moist forest environments. This small, inconspicuous fungus plays an important ecological role as a wood-decay organism in New England's temperate forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous Texture",
      "description": "Exhibits a translucent, gel-like consistency that is soft and watery, characteristic of jelly fungi in the Dacrymyces genus",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Corticioid Growth Form",
      "description": "Grows as a thin, spreading crust or patch on decaying wood rather than forming discrete fruiting bodies",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Yellow-Orange Coloration",
      "description": "Typically displays pale yellow to orange tones that may fade with age or moisture loss",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Dacrymyces corticioides thrives on decaying hardwood and coniferous logs in moist, shaded forest environments throughout New England, particularly in areas with high humidity.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like other jelly fungi, Dacrymyces corticioides can absorb and lose water rapidly, allowing it to revive from a dried, brittle state when moistened by rain.",
